   "subject": "Re: 'date.test. in detection histories",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi, <div>this error is unrelated to the error you had earlier. It arises because the camera operation matrix is not in the format expected by the function detectionHistory. I suppose you load the camera operation matrix from your hard disk after saving it? By default, saving and loading a data frame with the default settings will change the data frame. The row names will become a distinct column. That column has no name and therefore R calls it &quot;X&quot;, as happened in your case above.</div><div><br></div><div>You can do two different things:</div><div><br></div><div>1. run cameraOperation() anew and use the matrix the function gives you instead of an object that was saved as a file</div><div>2. load the file with two additional arguments:  <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">write.csv(..., <b>rownames = 1,</b><b> </b><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">check.names =</span> <span style=\"font-weight:bold\">FALSE</span>)</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><br></font></div><div><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">The package vignette also mentions this issue briefly: see <a href=\"https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/camtrapR/vignettes/DataExtraction.html#saving-and-loading-camera-operation-matrices\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/camtrapR/vignettes/DataExtraction.html%23saving-and-loading-camera-operation-matrices&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792461121443000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2MxweqYrOydzb3WpOeT6R5\">here</a>.</font></div><div><br></div><div>Best regards,</div><div>Jürgen</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
}
